window.docContents[197].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Commutative-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___2___5":{"contents":"Definition 2.2.5. An algebra A is commutative if ab = ba for all a, b \\in A.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Commutative algebra","header":"Commutative algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Commutative-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___2___5"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Commutativity-of-the-examples-in-2___2___4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Commutativity-of-the-examples-in-2___2___4--Commutativity-of-the-examples-in-2___2___4--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: AddMonoidAlgebra.commRing\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_commutativity_examples; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: CommRing\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_commutativity_examples; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: MvPolynomial\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_commutativity_examples; role=supporting\n\nFor a nontrivial commutative ring and a natural number greater than one, there are elements\nwith unequal products in opposite orders.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_commutativity_examples; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Commutativity of the examples in 2.2.4\u0009Commutativity of the examples in 2.2.4\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Commutativity-of-the-examples-in-2___2___4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Commutativity-of-the-examples-in-2___2___4--Commutativity-of-the-examples-in-2___2___4--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Left and right modules over commutative rings\u0009Left and right modules over commutative rings","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___2___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/#chapter-03___section-3-6":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory","header":"3.6. Characters of representations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/#chapter-03___section-3-6"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___8___-Character-tables___-examples--Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5--Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The displayed representation is isomorphic to the comparison representation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.8.1; role=primary]\n\nThe character of each indexed representation at each indexed group element is the corresponding\ncomplex table entry.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.8.1; role=primary]\n\nRepresentations with distinct indices are not isomorphic.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.8.1; role=primary]\n\nEvery representation in the indexed family is simple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.8.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.8. Character tables, examples\u0009Character tables of Q_8, S_4, and A_5\u0009Character tables of Q_8, S_4, and A_5\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___8___-Character-tables___-examples--Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5--Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___3___-Principal-series/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____--5___25___3___-Principal-series--Section-5___25___3___-Principal-series-representations-___-setup-of-B___-V____lambda_1___lambda_2___--Section-5___25___3___-Principal-series-representations-___-setup-of-B___-V____lambda_1___lambda_2___--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary finite-dimensional representation associated with a pair of monoid homomorphisms.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_5.25.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary paired representation has the displayed finite dimension.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_5.25.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.25.2; role=supporting]\n\nIdentifies the character of the auxiliary representation at the trivial unit character with a specified complex-valued function.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_5.25.3; role=supporting]\n\nIdentifies the character of the two-parameter auxiliary representation with trivial second parameter.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_5.25.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\u00095.25.3. Principal series\u0009Section 5.25.3: Principal series representations — setup of B, V_{lambda_1,lambda_2}\u0009Section 5.25.3: Principal series representations — setup of B, V_{lambda_1,lambda_2}\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___3___-Principal-series/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____--5___25___3___-Principal-series--Section-5___25___3___-Principal-series-representations-___-setup-of-B___-V____lambda_1___lambda_2___--Section-5___25___3___-Principal-series-representations-___-setup-of-B___-V____lambda_1___lambda_2___--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quiver-___D_4___/Section-6___3-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter6___Section6___3_heading":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.3. Indecomposable representations of the quiver $D_4$\u0009Section 6.3 heading and introduction","header":"Section 6.3 heading and introduction","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quiver-___D_4___/Section-6___3-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter6___Section6___3_heading"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___6___-Reflection-functors--Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex--Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The arrow type obtained by reversing arrows incident to a distinguished vertex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Definition6.6.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Definition 6.6.2: Reversed quiver at a vertex\u0009Definition 6.6.2: Reversed quiver at a vertex\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___6___-Reflection-functors--Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex--Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Corollary-6___8___3___-Uniqueness-of-indecomposable-with-given-dimension-vector/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem--Corollary-6___8___3___-Uniqueness-of-indecomposable-with-given-dimension-vector":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem","header":"Corollary 6.8.3: Uniqueness of indecomposable with given dimension vector","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Corollary-6___8___3___-Uniqueness-of-indecomposable-with-given-dimension-vector/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem--Corollary-6___8___3___-Uniqueness-of-indecomposable-with-given-dimension-vector"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-and-k-linear-functor/#Chapter7___Definition7___9___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Additive and k-linear functor\u0009Additive and k-linear functor","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-and-k-linear-functor/#Chapter7___Definition7___9___1___formalization"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Projective-resolution/#Chapter8___Definition8___2___1":{"contents":"Definition 8.2.1. A projective resolution of M is an exact sequence\n\n\\cdots \\to P_2 \\to P_1 \\to P_0 \\to M \\to 0\n\nsuch that all modules P_i, i \\geq 0, are projective.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Projective resolution","header":"Projective resolution","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Projective-resolution/#Chapter8___Definition8___2___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Projective-generator-_LPAR_progenerator_RPAR_/#Chapter9___Definition9___6___2":{"contents":"Definition 9.6.2. An object P of an abelian category \\mathcal{C} is said to be a projective generator (or progenerator) if it is projective and every object is a quotient of a multiple of P.\nFor example, in the category of modules over a ring A, the free module A is a projective generator.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Projective generator (progenerator)","header":"Projective generator (progenerator)","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Projective-generator-_LPAR_progenerator_RPAR_/#Chapter9___Definition9___6___2"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Basic-algebra":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.7. Morita equivalence","header":"Basic algebra","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Basic-algebra"}});